<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Constantine, Algeria</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cirta</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Constantine is a city in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, built on a rocky plateau cut by the gorge of the Rhumel River. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Cirta</a> it was the capital of the Numidian kings, and after it was rebuilt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a> in the 4th century it took his name. It was the seat of an Ottoman bey until the French captured it in 1837.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constantine-algeria.jpg" alt="Constantine, Algeria" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abdousid · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Constantine_bridge_in_spring.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
